it's been confirmed tonight that more  than a hundred and fifty thousand people  have now died in the uk after  contracting covid two years and one day  since covid19 was first identified in  china the uk has become the seventh  country to reach that terrible milestone  well here's the latest data released  just a short time ago another  146  390 new covid cases have been recorded  today as well as  313 new deaths within 28 days of a  positive test well that means the total  number of deaths now stands at a hundred  and fifty thousand  and fifty seven now political  correspondent rob powell is in  westminster for us tonight  rob  an extraordinary figure isn't it 150 057  deaths two years into a pandemic  pressure surely mounting on the  government now to respond to that but  they of course say that plan b was put  in place precisely to stop the spread of  this variant  yeah and i think what the government  will point to um when it comes to the  question over what happens next with  restrictions is the fact that whilst the  case numbers you outlined there  are far higher than they were back at  the january peak this time  last year the death rate is obviously  far lower this time last year we were  seeing um 1200 1300 deaths um that was  the peak per day this time last year  we're obviously at uh 300 today and some  figures that have been lower than that  in recent days despite confirmed  infections being the highest  they have ever  been so  whilst yes this is a sad milestone that  is being passed  i think the government will continue to  make the argument that the vaccination  programme and the booster campaign um is  bringing down death substantially and  indeed that's what the government has  said in a statement that they've put out  in response to this point being reached  in which a government spokesperson  has said that every death from this  virus is a tragedy and our sympathies  are with everyone who has lost loved  ones we are thankful for the collective  national effort and the hard work of  frontline health and social care staff  and volunteers for administering  vaccines to tens of millions of people  and keeping people safe their tireless  efforts have saved thousands of lives  but the pandemic is not over that's why  it's so important everyone continues to  play their part by coming forward to get  boosted now or getting a first or second  jab if you have yet to do so  now obviously the thing that has  radically changed how the government is  handling covid now is the vaccination  program the impact that has had  both on  hospitalizations and deaths so i think  that is part of the reason  why there is i think an increasingly  hardening view within government  about what uh bar needs to be passed in  order to justify  more restrictions i think just before  christmas your potential when less was  known about omicron as a variant and the  information the data was more uncertain  about its severity it did look like we  were on a path to potentially  tighter restrictions and potentially  curbs on indoor mixing i think we are in  something of a different place now  you've got a sense over the christmas  period that within the conservative  party and indeed within cabinet that  they put their foot down somewhat  around  going for more restrictions without  having the data there i think the  government has been buoyed somewhat by  data showing that yes whilst  hospitalizations  have increased  um recently the number of people  requiring the most intensive therapy and  intensive treatment has not necessarily  gone up at the same rate so i think that  is why at the moment the position the  government is in for england anyway  is potentially one that we may well not  see  further restrictions ramped up in the  short term um anyway all of that said i  think there are several big caveats to  that schools went back last week and  we're yet to see i think the impact that  that sort of mixing has on the case  rates and the hospitalization rates  we've potentially not quite seen  the full impact of the christmas and new  year period of mixing  on hospitalizations and deaths as well  so  it could all  need a course correction if that turns  out  to be bad but i think um as we reach 150  000 deaths there will be those that  potentially not today making this point  but will do in the future that of course  the impact of covid is not just counted  through people who lose their lives to  covet it's also counted through the  impact it has on everyone's lives on  their education on businesses that are  disrupted and i think that is why there  will need to be a very pressing case  made if we are going to see severe  restrictions put in again in the manner  that we have had in the last two years  yeah it is a complex uh picture isn't it  rob powell thanks very much indeed for  that
 

